The weird way this rental startup delivers its cars

If you were asked to imagine a driverless car, you=E2=80=99d probably think=
 of:

- An autonomous vehicle, like a Waymo

- A situation that=E2=80=99s gone very awry

With Vay, a Berlin-based startup that offers on-demand car rentals in Las V=
egas, it means a third thing: remotely controlled cars.

How does that work?

Vay=E2=80=99s fleet consists of 100 Kia Niro SUVs, per (https://www.busines=
sinsider.com/vay-startup-driverless-car-rental-service-remote-driving-2026-=
1?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz--BSohf344zXLSmK8gtc6TW8ZPrODHyBDyWv465nT2YW6FQrDrbV9fD4g=
kCsDHW1aGkmqIdXJyAtyVC0R25C7BWmyA_VA&_hsmi=3D400116481 ) Business Insider, =
which customers can order through its app.

-

Customers can pick up their car themselves, or a remote driver =E2=80=94 a =
human sitting in Vay=E2=80=99s office in front of several monitors, a steer=
ing wheel, and pedals =E2=80=94 can pilot it to the customer.

-

Once a customer receives the car, they take over driving and the remote dri=
ver is disconnected. Customers return cars in designated service zones or h=
and them back off to remote drivers.

Vincent Reddy, an operations lead, told BI that Vay=E2=80=99s remote drivin=
g setup is similar to a =E2=80=9Chigh-grade racing sim=E2=80=9D but without=
 the ability to feel the road or the car=E2=80=99s movement.

Unlike autonomous vehicles, which often use LiDAR to navigate, Vay uses onl=
y cameras. It=E2=80=99s a lot cheaper, and a human =E2=80=94 not a bot =E2=
=80=94 remains in control.

Vay=E2=80=99s cars can=E2=80=99t drive over 25 mph until renters take over,=
 and if there=E2=80=99s an emergency, remote drivers can push a red button =
to pull over.

Who uses this service?

Vay=E2=80=99s been operating in Vegas since January 2024  (https://techcrun=
ch.com/2025/11/10/remote-driving-startup-vay-could-grab-up-to-410m-from-sin=
gapores-grab/?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz--BSohf344zXLSmK8gtc6TW8ZPrODHyBDyWv465nT2YW6=
FQrDrbV9fD4gkCsDHW1aGkmqIdXJyAtyVC0R25C7BWmyA_VA&_hsmi=3D400116481 ) and sa=
ys it has provided 35k trips since its founding.

Vay aims to be cheaper and more convenient than Uber, charging 35 cents per=
 minute while driving and 5 cents per minute while parked.

The Las Vegas Review-Journal estimated (https://www.reviewjournal.com/busin=
ess/remote-rental-car-delivery-startup-expanding-las-vegas-operations-33189=
92/?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz--BSohf344zXLSmK8gtc6TW8ZPrODHyBDyWv465nT2YW6FQrDrbV9fD=
4gkCsDHW1aGkmqIdXJyAtyVC0R25C7BWmyA_VA&_hsmi=3D400116481 ) that a trip from=
 UNLV to the Hoover Dam and back, with a one-hour stopover, would cost ~$32=
. About half of Vay=E2=80=99s trips include a stopover =E2=80=94 which make=
s sense considering it=E2=80=99s probably easier to use a service like this=
 than call multiple Ubers to run a day=E2=80=99s worth of errands.

